  "question": {
    "text": "Consider two polaroids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> as shown. Unpolarised light is incident on polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>. Now, both the polaroids are rotated simultaneously by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></math> in the same sense of rotation such that at every instant, their pass (transmission) axes always remain parallel to each other. During the rotation, intensity of transmitted light through polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math><br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/335299q.png\" style=\"width: 191px; height: 136px;\" />",
    "image": null
  },
